          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+U%281%29-gauge+field"} higher U(1)-gauge field
            given by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+n-bundle+with+connection"} circle n-bundle with connection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (P, \nabla)
            over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+manifold"} (pseudo)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,g)
            is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mi g
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\nabla \mapsto \int_X F_\nabla \wedge \star_g F_{\nabla}
                  \\,,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_\nabla
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            = succeed "-form." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi dim
                  %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mn 4
                  %mi k
                  %mo +
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  dim X = 4 k + 2
          %p
            then the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+star"} Hodge star
            operator squares to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} +1
            (Lorentzian signature) or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -1
            (Euclidean signature) on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^{k+1}(X)
            %em self-duality
            or
            %em chirality
            constraint
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ±
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo =
                  %mo ⋆
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\pm F_\nabla = \star F_\nabla
                  \\,.
          %p
            With this duality constraint imposed, one speaks of
            %strong self-dual higher gauge fields
            or
            %strong chiral higher gauge fields
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %strong higher gauge fields with self-dual curvature
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            are called
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+instanton"} Yang-Mills instanton
            field configurations.)
          %p
            Since imposing the self-duality constraint on the fields that enter the above
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            makes that functional vanish identically, self-dual higher gauge theory is notorious for being subtle in that either it does not have a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+field+theory"} Lagrangian field theory
            description, or else a somewhat intricate indirect one (e.g.
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Witten96"} Witten 96, p. 7
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#BelovMooreI"} Belov-Moore 06a
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Witten96"} Witten 96, p.8
            %a{:href => "#Witten99"} Witten 99, section 3
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#MW00"} DMW 00b, page 3
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polarization"} polarization
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            of an unconstrained theory in one dimension higher. By such as “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle"}> holographic principle
            ” the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+function"} partition function
            of the self-dual theory on an
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            of dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 4
                  %mi k
                  %mo +
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 4 k +2
            is given by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state"} state
            (wave function) of an abelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+dimensional+Chern-Simons+theory"} higher dimensional Chern-Simons theory
            in dimension
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 4
                    %mi k
                    %mo +
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 4 k + 3
          %p
            The way this works is understood in much mathematical detail for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k = 0
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS3-CFT2+and+CS-WZW+correspondence"} AdS3-CFT2 and CS-WZW correspondence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k = 1
            (
            %a{:href => "#Witten96"}> Witten 96
            ,
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quadratic+Functions+in+Geometry%2C+Topology%2C+and+M-Theory"} Hopkins-Singer 02
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-brane"} M5-brane
            and
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/6d+%282%2C0%29-superconformal+QFT"} 6d (2,0)-superconformal QFT
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "AdS-CFT#AdS7CFT6"} AdS7/CFT6
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+fields"} higher gauge fields
            are
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycle"} cocycle
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+differential+cohomology"} ordinary differential cohomology
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#MW00"} DMW 00
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#BelovMooreII"} Belov-Moore 06b
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+K-theory"} differential
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+K-theory"} complex K-theory
            produces the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-fields"} RR-fields
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+superstring+theory"} type II superstring theory
            in dimension 10.
          %h2#Definition Definition (outline)
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle"} holographic
            definition of self-dual abelian higher gauge field theory in dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 4
                  %mi k
                  %mo +
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 4k+2
            given in (
            %a{:href => "#Witten96"}> Witten 96
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Witten99"} Witten 99
          %p
            Imagine the self-dual
            = succeed "-form" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2k
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            to be coupled to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sources"} sources
            given by
            = succeed "-form" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mi k
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (2k+1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycles"} cocycles
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+differential+cohomology"} ordinary differential cohomology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %msubsup
                    %mi 𝔾
                    %mi conn
                    %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\Sigma, \mathbf{B}^{2k+1}\mathbb{G}^\times_{conn}]
            of degree
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mi k
                    %mo +
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (2k+2)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
            for this coupling (and that is what is discussed in (
            %a{:href => "#BelovMooreI"}> Belov-Moore 06I
            )) and the resulting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+function"} partition function
            is then locally a function of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/source+fields"} source fields
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intermediate+Jacobian"} intermediate Jacobian
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %msubsup
                    %mi 𝔾
                    %mi conn
                    %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\Sigma, \mathbf{B}^{2k+1}\mathbb{G}^\times_{conn}]
            and globally (since the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            will not be strictly
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+invariance"} gauge invariant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            of a certain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holomorphic+line+bundle"} holomorphic line bundle
            on that space.
          %p
            However, no choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
            will enforce a strictly self-dual gauge field (the anti-self dual component will remain in the space of fields, hence the partition function will depend on it). Therefore the idea is to turn this around, reject the idea that self-dual higher gauge theory is directly itself a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+quantum+field+theory"} Lagrangian quantum field theory
            %em define
            the partition function of the self-dual higher gauge theory (and thereby, indirectly, the self-dual higher gauge theory itself!) as being a certain section of a certain line bundle on the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intermediate+Jacobian"} intermediate Jacobian
          %p
            The question then is: which line bundle? Some plausibility arguments show that it must be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Theta+characteristic"} Theta characteristic
            bundle, hence a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/square+root"} square root
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+bundle"} canonical bundle
            on the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intermediate+Jacobian"} intermediate Jacobian
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theta+functions"} theta functions
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+function"} partition function
          %p
            The claim then is that the correct choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Theta+characteristic"} Theta characteristic
            to use is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quadratic+refinement"} quadratic refinement
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersection+pairing"} intersection pairing
            (the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Beilinson-Deligne+cup-product"} Beilinson-Deligne cup-product
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %msubsup
                    %mi 𝔾
                    %mi conn
                    %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %msubsup
                    %mi 𝔾
                    %mi conn
                    %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mo ∪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mn 4
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 3
                  %msubsup
                    %mi 𝔾
                    %mi conn
                    %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mo ∫
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msubsup
                    %mi 𝔾
                    %mi conn
                    %mo ×
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [\Sigma, \mathbf{B}^{2k+1}\mathbb{G}^\times_{conn}]
                  \\times
                  [\Sigma, \mathbf{B}^{2k+1}\mathbb{G}^\times_{conn}]
                  \\stackrel{\cup}{\longrightarrow}
                  [\Sigma, \mathbf{B}^{4k+3}\mathbb{G}^\times_{conn}]
                  \\stackrel{\int}{\longrightarrow}
                  \\mathbf{B}\mathbb{G}^\times_{conn}
                  \\,.
          %p
            Such
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quadratic+refinement"} quadratic refinement
            turns out to be given for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k = 0
            by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spin+structure"} Spin structure
            (leading to “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spin+Chern-Simons+theory"}> Spin Chern-Simons theory
            ”) and for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k =1
            by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wu+structure"} Wu structure
            (and the latter case is the actual example of interest in (
            %a{:href => "#Witten96"}> Witten 96
            )). This is what the central theorem of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quadratic+Functions+in+Geometry%2C+Topology%2C+and+M-Theory"}> Hopkins-Singer 02
            ) establishes rigorously.
          %p
            One notices now that while the self-dual
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 4
                    %mi k
                    %mo +
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (4k+2)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+quantum+field+theory"} Lagrangian quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle"} holographically
            form a field theory in dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 4
                  %mi k
                  %mo +
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 4k+3
            that is, namely the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersection+pairing"} intersection pairing
            above is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+dimensional+Chern-Simons+theory"} higher dimensional Chern-Simons theory
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holomorphic+line+bundle"} holomorphic line bundle
            which it gives rise to on the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intermediate+Jacobian"} intermediate Jacobian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
            of Chern-Simons theory, whose holomorphic sections are its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
            (see at
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization+of+Chern-Simons+theory"} quantization of Chern-Simons theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/square+root"} square root
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quadratic+refinement"} quadratic refinement
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metaplectic+correction"} metaplectic correction
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            of Chern-Simobs theory.
          %p This kind of relation
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th
                  self-dual
                  = succeed "d" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 4
                          %mi k
                          %mo +
                          %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (4k+2)
                %th
                %th
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 4
                        %mi k
                        %mo +
                        %mn 3
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (4k+3)
                  d Chern-Simons theory
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sources"} sources
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
